2026-11-09  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.7.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.6.0:

    New features:
        - Give the OS an RTC and CMOS RAM of its own: the machine's clock
          where the firmware's FADT allows it and the RTC reads sanely, else
          an emulated one set from the firmware's time, and CMOS RAM that no
          longer reaches the machine's.
        - Give the OS the PC/AT's 8237 DMA controllers, a reset control
          register at CF9h of its own, and, for OSes that clear CR0.NE, the
          PC/AT's report of x87 errors on IRQ13, with port F0h.
        - Give the OS the serial and parallel ports that the firmware's ACPI
          describes, with their interrupts, including those the OS turns on
          or moves, but for the one ReBIOS logs to. The other legacy ports
          read as absent, as on a board without those devices.
        - Describe ReBIOS's legacy devices in the OS's ACPI namespace in place
          of the firmware's: the 8259s, the 8254, the RTC, the speaker's port,
          the 8237s, the x87 error port, and the PS/2 keyboard and mouse.
        - Take the serial log's port from the firmware's SPCR or DBG2 table:
          `serial_port` defaults to `auto`.
        - For OSes without ACPI, do what the firmware does outside ACPI mode:
          power the machine off at a press of the power button, a
          control-method one too, and watch the firmware's thermal zones,
          powering the machine off past their critical and hot trip points,
          running their fans past their active ones, and slowing their
          processors down past their passive ones, until the OS's own ACPI
          takes them over.
        - Give the OS a PM timer of its own, counted from the TSC, where the
          machine's stands still, goes backwards, or runs at the wrong rate.
        - Run the machine's local APICs in x2APIC mode wherever the processor
          has it and the firmware's DMAR table does not opt out of it, with
          the IOMMU remapping interrupts to x2APIC destinations, choosing the
          mode once at boot rather than following the OS's.
        - With `save_log`, keep ReBIOS's log in EFI variables while the OS
          runs, for machines without a serial port: the next start of ReBIOS
          writes it to `rebios-last.log` beside `rebios.efi`.
        - Report the DMA faults that the IOMMUs record while the OS runs, not
          only while the legacy BIOS starts.
        - Make the emulated VGA several times faster: repeated string
          instructions on its memory, and repeated OUTS from RAM, run in bulk
          rather than an iteration at a time, its writes reach all four
          planes at once, MOVSB copies through its latches go a run at a
          time, and its redraws take a half to a fifth of the time. Every
          emulated memory access also reads less of the processor's state.
        - Load the microcode updates that the OS gives the processor, on
          Intel's and AMD's processors alike, as the OS would on bare metal,
          which a hypervisor's guest cannot do itself.
        - Show the OS the BARs it gave a device wherever the device gives its
          configuration space a second way: the copies that NVIDIA's and AMD's
          GPUs keep in their BARs and reach through their ports, and the
          RTL8168's window onto its MSI-X table.

    Bug fixes:
        - Reach the machine's legacy devices only where its firmware describes
          them, and its quirks allow: mask the 8259s only where the MADT has
          them, sound the speaker only where ACPI has the 8254 and port 61h
          and the 8254 counts, probe the 8042 only as Linux 7.3 does, pulse
          its reset line only where one exists, and use CF9h only where the
          PCI configuration ports answer, with Linux 7.3's values. Port 92h
          and the PCH's 8254 gate are left alone.
        - Run the firmware's AML and ReBIOS's own accesses to the RTC through
          one owner, so that neither changes the index register under the
          other.
        - Split wide port accesses to 8-bit devices into bytes, as an ISA bus
          does.
        - Pass the OS only the interrupt lines of the devices it gets, and
          follow its HPET's legacy replacement route, which IRQs 0 and 8
          take.
        - Raise INIT from the keyboard controller's reset line, as the
          chipset does: the legacy BIOS then resumes where the CMOS shutdown
          byte asks it to, or resets the machine.
        - Fill the OS's A20-masked view of memory as it reaches it, rather
          than all at once when the gate first closes, which kept the
          processor from the OS for tens of milliseconds.
        - Check the firmware's tables and the hardware before trusting them,
          as Linux 7.3 does: memory map entries that overlap, or that are
          specific-purpose or uncacheable, SMBIOS 2.1 entry points of 1Eh
          bytes, broken XSDTs, I/O APICs that read all ones or share GSIs,
          processors whose APIC IDs an IPI cannot name alone, VT-d units at
          address 0 or reading all ones, the interrupt remapping erratum of
          Intel's 5500, 5520, and X58 I/O hubs, pre-2016 firmware's ECAM
          reservations, the DMAR table's x2APIC opt-out, the TSC-deadline
          erratum of older microcode, and the C1E timer erratum of AMD's
          family 10h.
        - Measure the TSC's rate against the HPET or the PM timer, and take
          the processor's nominal rate where it agrees, rather than timing it
          with the firmware's Stall() alone.
        - Leave alone GPUs that have no display attached, whose firmware can
          hang when set to a mode.
        - Give the OS the firmware's EDID only as Linux 7.3 would take it,
          else one of ReBIOS's own.
        - Flush the whole ASID for the INVLPGA of the OS's own hypervisors,
          as Linux 7.3's KVM does for INVLPGA's errata on Genoa and Turin.
        - Hand the legacy BIOS the SMBIOS table's length and address in full,
          whose upper bytes came from uninitialised memory.
        - Set up every processor's control registers, PAT, and MTRRs alike,
          whatever the firmware left on each: the others could not call the
          firmware's runtime services, which may use SSE, shut down at
          machine checks, and kept MTRRs unlike the boot processor's.
        - Start the processors one at a time, only those that the MADT has
          enabled, with INIT and SIPIs of their own rather than broadcast
          ones: firmware marks disabled the local APICs that hang the
          machine when written to.
        - Restore the firmware's LVT entries when leaving x2APIC mode, take
          each LINT pin's NMI from the MADT as ACPI ranks its structures,
          fence the writes to x2APIC registers, select I/O APIC registers
          with byte writes, leave the firmware's SMI entries alone, and clear
          the remote IRRs that unacknowledged interrupts left set.
        - Give PCI interrupt links that the firmware left disabled an IRQ, as
          Linux 7.3 does, and take the SCI as active low and level-triggered
          where the MADT does not say otherwise.
        - End a call to the firmware's runtime services that takes a page
          fault, and call them no more, rather than panicking, as Linux 7.3
          does.
        - Quiesce the invalidation queue that the firmware left on in each
          VT-d unit, stop each AMD-Vi unit's command buffer and logs before
          the unit, as their specifications ask, and add the I/O APICs that
          some laptops' IVRS tables leave out, as Linux 7.3 does.
        - Disconnect the firmware's graphics output rather than its graphics
          driver, which left some panels dark.
        - Probe serial ports by their divisor latch, turn on their FIFOs, and
          take the UART's clock from the SPCR.
        - Allocate no memory after the first ExitBootServices() call, as UEFI
          requires, align boot sector reads as the drive asks, and clear the
          firmware's CPUID limit before anything reads CPUID.
        - Work around AMD's processor errata as Linux 7.3 does: its chicken
          bits, a cleared divider before every entry to the OS on Zen 1, no
          virtualised VMLOAD and VMSAVE on the Zen 4 models where they reset
          the machine, and a #UD in the OS rather than a panic for an INVLPGB
          that the processor refuses.
        - Turn on the decoding of a PCI serial card that ReBIOS logs to and of
          the card whose VBIOS runs, which firmware without drivers for them
          leaves off.
        - Drop the OS's translations on INIT and when it turns paging on, on
          Intel's processors, which kept Windows 10 from starting its other
          processors.
        - Emulate the PC's interrupt controllers as Linux 7.3's KVM does: keep
          the 8259s' in-service bits across ICW1, and pass their interrupts to
          local APIC LINT0 entries that ask for an NMI, as old Linux's NMI
          watchdog has them; clear an I/O APIC entry's Remote IRR when it is
          made edge-triggered; and in the local APICs, deliver nothing to one
          that is globally disabled, give a lowest priority interrupt to one
          that takes interrupts, take every INIT but the level de-assert,
          raise #GP for reserved bits of x2APIC mode's ICR and SELF IPI, count
          a late periodic timer's next period, and match physical destinations
          by the whole APIC ID.
        - Release the 8254's latched count when the counter is reprogrammed,
          as its datasheet has it.

    Miscellaneous:
        - Share the definitions and helpers that files repeated, and include
          only the headers that each file uses.
        - `make run` runs Nephrite, the fork of QEMU that ReBIOS is tested
          on, in place of QEMU, and emulates an Intel processor with VMX
          behind VT-d, or with `QEMU_VIRT=svm` an AMD one with SVM behind
          AMD-Vi, rather than nesting KVM.

2026-11-07  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.6.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.5.2:

    New features:
        - The OS can run hypervisors of its own, such as Linux's KVM, on AMD
          processors too: ReBIOS gives it the processor's SVM, with nested
          paging, NRIP saving, VMCB clean bits, flush by ASID, PAUSE
          filtering, LBR virtualisation, read-only guest page tables, page
          modification logging, TlbiCtl, decode assists, NMI virtualisation,
          the virtual GIF, virtual VMLOAD and VMSAVE, the guest mode execute
          trap, the TSC ratio MSR, SVM-Lock, the idle HLT intercept, the bus
          lock threshold, and FRED virtualisation, each where the processor
          has it, and with the guest VMCB address check.
        - On AMD processors, run the VMLOAD, VMSAVE, CLGI, and STGI of the
          OS's own hypervisors on the processor where it virtualises them,
          and have HLT exit to ReBIOS only where no interrupt waits for the
          OS, which saves exits.
        - The OS always gets the processor's VMX on Intel processors: the
          `nested` setting is obsolete, and ignored with a warning. ReBIOS
          sets aside 1/32 of the memory, at most 128 MiB, for the page tables
          it runs the guests of the OS's own hypervisors with.
        - Give the OS FRED, user interrupts, and, on Intel processors, APX,
          where the processor has them.
        - Give the OS INVLPGB and TLBSYNC, PCID, and the extended APIC space
          with its extended interrupt LVTs, on AMD processors that have them.
        - Post the OS's MSIs to its processors through the IOMMU where it can:
          with VT-d's posted interrupts, with IPI virtualisation or without,
          and with AMD-Vi's guest virtual APIC mode where AVIC runs.
        - On machines with several PCI host bridges or segments, show the OS
          one host bridge, with the other root buses behind ports on its bus
          0, so that OSes that know only one still find every device. The
          legacy BIOS follows the firmware's boot order for drives on those
          buses too, and _OSC grants the OS only what every host bridge does.
        - Make the OS's MCFG, MADT, and HPET tables describe the machine it
          gets rather than adjust the firmware's: one PCI segment, only the
          processors and I/O APICs it has, and no hidden HPET.
        - Keep the OS's port 92h in ReBIOS: its fast reset bit now sends INIT
          to the OS's processors, as the chipset's INIT# does, rather than
          reaching the machine's own.
        - On AMD processors with HOST_MCE_OVERRIDE, log the banks of a
          machine check that shuts down an OS without CR4.MCE.

    Bug fixes:
        - Keep the machine's page 0 out of the OS's memory: the OS's own
          hypervisors failed where it held their data structures, which
          ReBIOS took for missing.
        - Raise #UD, as the processor does, for the SVM instructions that an
          OS runs outside ring 0 without enabling SVM, rather than #GP.
        - Keep AMD's extended APIC registers when ReBIOS's own local APIC
          leaves x2APIC mode, so that its APIC IDs stay 8 bits wide.
        - Update Tendril, which now waits for USB devices to show up after an
          xHCI controller's reset.
        - Update SeaBIOS, which now reads a disc swapped into a CD drive
          rather than fail every read of it.

    Miscellaneous:
        - Align the code with the updated style rules.
        - Panic on processors that are neither Intel's nor AMD's, and on the
          other processor vendor's IOMMU, which only virtual machines have.

2026-11-03  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.5.2 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.5.1:

    New features:
        - All of ReBIOS, SeaBIOS included, now builds with Clang, LLD, and
          LLVM's tools with `TOOLCHAIN=llvm`, and with gold: a `-fuse-ld=`
          option in `LDFLAGS` now picks SeaBIOS's linker too.
        - The emulated VGA's video BIOS is now a small 8086 program in a 13 KiB
          ROM, down from 32 KiB, with the rest in the hypervisor instead of in
          16-bit C. The usual text calls run in the ROM, and in true real mode
          everything else takes a single VM exit, so both are much faster.
          Under V86, some calls, such as AX=4F01h, now take more VM exits than
          before.

    Bug fixes:
        - Give Windows x64, which runs a video BIOS in an x86 emulator, the
          right VBE modes: the old video BIOS listed them twice there, and with
          Clang not at all, which kept an installed Windows 7 at 16 colours.
        - Put the emulated VGA's linear framebuffer in the memory of the bridge
          above the VGA's card: an installed Windows 7 stayed at 16 colours on
          a card behind a bridge, as AMD's integrated graphics are.
        - Keep Windows Vista from crashing at boot (STOP 0x7E) when a driver
          gives a forwarded ACPI method an empty string, which ReBIOS's ACPI
          tables stored in their mailbox.
        - Date the BIOS 12/31/99, so that Windows 98 SE's Setup installs ACPI
          by itself: with an earlier date it did so only with `/p j`, and else
          found no PCI bus.
        - Add INT 10h AH=1Ch, which the video BIOS's functionality table
          already claimed.

    Miscellaneous:
        - The `SEABIOS_OBJDUMP` and `VGABIOS_*` make variables are gone, as
          nothing uses them any more.
        - `make clean` no longer needs a compiler for SeaBIOS.
        - Update SeaBIOS for building with Clang, LLD, and gold, and for the
          BIOS date, with a `CONFIG_BIOS_DATE` option. CI checks the gold
          builds too.

2026-11-02  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.5.1 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.5.0:

    New features:
        - `vga_emulation = never` now runs a video BIOS wherever there is one,
          even on graphics whose video BIOS ReBIOS would skip by default.

    Bug fixes:
        - Decide by lists of PCI IDs whose video BIOS to run by default, as
          those of recent integrated graphics can hang the machine. Cards in
          slots, now including AMD's RDNA ones, and graphics in the processor
          or soldered to the board that came out before 2018 run theirs. Those
          from 2018 on, now including AMD's Vega ones, get the emulated VGA.
          Graphics on neither list count as a card in a slot only if the
          machine has no battery, neither its ACPI power profile nor its SMBIOS
          chassis type rules slots out, and a PCI Express port above them has a
          slot.
        - Power off through ACPI's S5 state before the firmware's service, as
          Linux 7.3 does and as restarts already did: the service of a Dell
          Inspiron 7700 crashes by then.
        - Keep small prefetchable BARs in their bridge's prefetchable window,
          as firmware does, wherever that window is below 4 GiB, instead of in
          its memory window.
        - Where VM exits are slow, as under another hypervisor, hold the next
          timer tick through the 8259 until the OS has had as long as the last
          one took, for the 8254's ticks and for those of the HPET's legacy
          replacement route. A tick could otherwise come before what the last
          one interrupted ran again, over and over, which stalled Linux's boot.
          Elsewhere, ticks follow the counter alone.

2026-10-31  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.5.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.4.1:

    New features:
        - Emulate the 8254 timer on every machine, so that the OS and the
          legacy BIOS have a working one whether or not the chipset's runs. The
          chipset's now only sounds the speaker.
        - Emulate the 8042 keyboard controller on every machine. PS/2 devices
          plugged into a real 8042 keep working through it, and USB keyboards
          and mice reach the OS and the legacy BIOS as PS/2 ones. The ACPI
          tables show its devices where the firmware does not.
        - Drive xHCI, EHCI, OHCI, and UHCI controllers with the Tendril library
          until the OS takes them, with the handoff a BIOS's legacy USB support
          has, so that an OS without a USB driver keeps its keyboard and mouse.
        - Give the legacy BIOS the USB disks through the hypervisor, for
          booting from them and for INT 13h, in place of SeaBIOS's own USB
          stack.
        - New `legacy_usb` setting, `true` by default, for driving the USB
          controllers: with `false` they are left to the OS, and the legacy
          BIOS has no USB.
        - New `avic` setting for when AMD's AVIC runs the OS's local APICs:
          `auto`, the default, on Zen 4 and later processors with x2AVIC;
          `always`, on every processor that has it but those AMD advises
          against it on; or `never`.

    Bug fixes:
        - Restart through ACPI's reset register, the keyboard controller, or
          the reset control register before the firmware's reset service, as
          Linux 7.3 does: the service of a Dell Inspiron 7700 crashes by then.
        - Use AVIC by default only on Zen 4 and later processors with x2AVIC,
          as Linux 7.3's KVM does: Windows hung at its logo with AVIC on a
          Zen 3+ processor without x2AVIC, such as the Ryzen 7 7735HS.
        - Deliver an interrupt already on its way when the OS masks its source
          on the vector the OS programmed, not on one of ReBIOS's own, which
          the OS has no handler for.
        - Hold the OS up less while the emulated VGA redraws: a redraw runs a
          millisecond at a time between the OS's own work, takes at most a
          quarter of the boot processor's time, and, in VBE modes, looks only
          at the lines on pages the OS wrote to.
        - Hold the OS up less while the firmware's AML runs, for an ACPI call
          of the OS's or for a firmware event: it too runs a millisecond at a
          time between the OS's own work.

    Miscellaneous:
        - The OS's local APIC timers are kept in software, as the processor's
          own timer is now the hypervisor's.
        - The log reports VM exits that keep a processor from the OS for more
          than 5 ms.
        - The SeaBIOS banner shows the ReBIOS version, taken from Git or, in
          release tarballs, from a `.version` file.
        - Follow the latest PicoEFI C template, and update the
          freestanding-c-hdrs subproject.
        - `make run` gives QEMU a USB keyboard and mouse on xHCI, as the
          template does.
        - Update SeaBIOS to its latest upstream revision, without its USB
          stack, and add the Tendril subproject.

