    *** Release 0.4.1 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.4.0:

    New features:
        - Panels mounted sideways in their casing: ReBIOS shows its log and the
          emulated VGA's picture upright on the machines whose panel Linux
          knows to be mounted so, such as the Lenovo IdeaPad D330-10IGM and the
          Steam Deck, and the emulated video BIOS offers VBE modes and an EDID
          of the upright screen's size.

    Bug fixes:
        - Correct the framebuffer's size, which some firmware gives as
          landscape on portrait panels, as Linux does: the Lenovo IdeaPad
          D330-10IGM and IdeaPad Duet 3 10IGL5, and the Steam Deck. ReBIOS's
          log was garbled on them.
        - Emulate the VGA by default on panels mounted sideways, rather than
          run the graphics card's video BIOS, which draws sideways and turned
          the Lenovo IdeaPad D330-10IGM's screen off.
        - In PIC mode, keep PCI devices' interrupts off IRQs 6 and 14, which
          the legacy BIOS unmasks for its floppy and ATA drivers on any
          machine: a PCI device's level-triggered interrupt there flooded it,
          and it got no further than its "Machine UUID" line.
        - Ungate the 8254 timer on Apollo Lake, Gemini Lake, Elkhart Lake,
          Jasper Lake, and Alder Lake-N chipsets too, whose firmware can switch
          its clock off: the legacy BIOS then waited forever for its timer
          interrupt after its "Machine UUID" line.

2026-10-26  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.4.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.3.0:

    New features:
        - Intel integrated graphics from Skylake on: the OS gets the GPU's
          stolen memory at an address in its own memory map, which the GPU's
          registers then report, and the firmware's graphics page table entries
          move with it, as the IOMMU translates what the GPU accesses. The OS's
          memory no longer has a hole where the firmware put the stolen memory.
          Setting the new `igpu_stolen_remap` to `false` keeps the stolen
          memory at the firmware's address, as before, should the screen go
          black once the OS starts.
        - New `cpuid_limit` setting, which starts the OS with CPUID reporting
          no basic leaf past 2, as firmware setups' "Limit CPUID Maxval" option
          does, for OSes that fail on processors with more, such as Windows NT
          4.0.
        - Panics show more of the processor's state: its APIC ID, where in
          ReBIOS it stopped, the control registers, DR6 and DR7, the
          general-purpose registers, the top of the stack, and the OS's last VM
          exit.
        - With `verbose` on, ReBIOS shows its log on the screen for 30 seconds
          before it resets the machine because the OS triple faulted or got
          into a state that the processor cannot run it in.

    Bug fixes:
        - Take the legacy video BIOS only from the graphics card that the
          firmware displays on, or from the one the `vga` setting names, and
          emulate the VGA if that card has none, rather than run another
          card's.
        - Keep the firmware's graphics driver running while emulating the VGA:
          stopping it turned some screens off, such as a 2019 iMac's.
        - Emulate the VGA by default under Apple's firmware, rather than run
          the graphics card's video BIOS, which nothing vouches for on Macs: a
          2019 iMac's screen went dark with it.
        - Intel integrated graphics: give the OS the OpRegion, with the video
          BIOS table that describes the GPU's outputs, at an address in its own
          memory map, where the GPU's ASLS register now points. ASLS pointed at
          the firmware's address, which in the OS's memory map is other memory,
          so that graphics drivers found no video BIOS table (new in 0.2.0).
        - Let the OS reach BARs smaller than a page, such as the 2 KiB ones of
          some AHCI controllers, wherever it moves them. They could read as all
          ones and ignore writes.
        - Take the USB controllers from the firmware before the legacy BIOS
          drives them, as Linux does at boot, xHCI ones included, and drop the
          IOMMU memory reservations that only the firmware's USB code needed.
        - Give the OS a TSC of its own, offset from the processor's, rather
          than let it write the processor's, which ReBIOS keeps time with:
          Windows XP zeroes it at boot. IA32_TSC_ADJUST and TSC deadlines
          follow the OS's TSC.
        - Intel: write IA32_MCG_CTL to the processor only as all 0s or all 1s,
          the values Intel defines, and give the OS back the value it wrote,
          such as Windows NT 4.0's 0xffffffff.
        - Intel: keep frame buffer compression off on Skylake and Broxton
          integrated graphics, as Linux does where VT-d translates the GPU's
          accesses, which it always does under ReBIOS.

    Miscellaneous:
        - Refuse to start, with a notice, as without an IOMMU, where the
          integrated graphics malfunctions under VT-d, as Linux knows it to:
          Intel's Broadwell, and Ironlake in Arrandale processors, or in
          Clarkdale ones whose firmware sets up no shadow GTT.
        - The emulated VGA redraws only the scan lines the OS wrote to, rather
          than whole screens.
        - Follow the latest PicoEFI C template, and update the PicoEFI,
          freestanding-c-hdrs, and freestanding-lgrt subprojects.

2026-10-20  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.3.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.2.0:

    New features:
        - Nested virtualisation on Intel processors: with the new `nested`
          setting on, the OS can use VMX itself to run virtual machines of its
          own, e.g. with Linux's KVM, with EPT, VPID, VMCS shadowing, APIC
          virtualisation and posted interrupts, the VMX-preemption timer,
          page-modification logging, TSC scaling, EPTP switching, and the other
          VMX features the processor has. It stays off by default until AMD's
          SVM is nested as well.
        - Let the processor's local APIC virtualisation run the OS's local
          APICs: on Intel, TPR shadowing, APIC-access and APIC-register
          virtualisation, virtual-interrupt delivery, posted interrupts, x2APIC
          virtualisation, and IPI virtualisation; on AMD, AVIC and x2AVIC.
          Local APIC accesses, such as 32-bit Windows' TPR writes, rarely stop
          the OS anymore: Windows 10 installs in half the time it took under
          nested KVM.
        - AMD: use flush by ASID, VMCB clean bits, decode assists, and virtual
          NMIs and GIF where the processor has them.
        - Interrupts that the OS programs into devices (MSIs and MSI-X entries)
          and into the I/O APICs arrive on vectors that ReBIOS hands out, which
          it then delivers as the OS asked: whatever the OS writes, no device
          interrupts ReBIOS's own vectors, and the NMIs and INITs the OS asks
          for reach its own processors only. Where the IOMMUs can, VT-d or
          AMD-Vi interrupt remapping carries these interrupts and blocks the
          ones devices send otherwise, e.g. by DMA.
        - Pass the processor's thermal and performance monitoring interrupts,
          and Intel's hardware feedback table, to the OS, so that its scheduler
          gets the processor's hints (Intel Thread Director).
        - Let the OS use Intel PT where its output can go through EPT, PEBS
          where its records can, MPX, LAM, and AMD's ERAPS, Upper Address
          Ignore, and LBR virtualisation.
        - Show ReBIOS's log, and any panic, on the screen with Flanterm.

    Bug fixes:
        - Map the framebuffer wherever the firmware put it: where that was
          above 4 GiB, as for many dedicated graphics cards, the emulated VGA
          stopped ReBIOS before showing anything (new in 0.2.0).
        - Bounce the BIOS's NVMe transfers to buffers that are not dword
          aligned, which the controller cannot take. FreeDOS did not boot from
          NVMe drives.
        - Emulated VGA: put the linear framebuffer in PCI memory that no device
          uses rather than in the BAR of a PCI function of ReBIOS's own.
          Windows 8 and later saw two display adapters, drawing both into the
          same screen.
        - Carry out hardware task switches as the processor does: their steps
          in its order, privilege checks for the new task's data segments,
          double faults for faults while an exception's switch runs, the stack
          limit for its error code, the EXT bit, the T flag's debug trap, and
          the reserved EFLAGS bits.
        - Walk the OS's paging as the processor does where ReBIOS accesses its
          memory: accessed and dirty flags, reserved bits, SMEP, SMAP,
          protection keys, and LASS.
        - Decode 32-bit real mode code (CS.D set in real mode) as 32-bit code,
          and check every iteration of the string instructions ReBIOS carries
          out against its segment's limit and rights.
        - Wrap RIP at 4 GiB outside 64-bit mode after the instructions ReBIOS
          carries out.
        - Debuggers work across the accesses ReBIOS carries out: data and I/O
          breakpoints on emulated memory and ports, RF as the processor keeps
          it, branch single-stepping (BTF), and the effects of debug traps'
          delivery on DR7 and DEBUGCTL.
        - Raise #GP for the control register writes the processor refuses, such
          as paging without protected mode, rather than reset, and flush the
          OS's TLB after the ones ReBIOS carries out.
        - Hand the OS what ReBIOS does not expect as the processor would,
          rather than stop: unknown instructions' exits as #UD, states that
          cannot be entered as a triple fault, code in emulated memory, and
          interrupt delivery onto a stack in it, as in VGA memory.
        - Keep the CPUID limit and the XD disable bit of IA32_MISC_ENABLE to
          the OS, which could otherwise limit ReBIOS's own CPUID or break its
          VM exits.
        - AMD: keep the OS's memory out of the HyperTransport address range,
          where the IOMMU and the processor give addresses special meanings.
        - AMD: give the OS a memory configuration of its own (SYSCFG, TOP_MEM,
          TOP_MEM2, IORRs, MMIO_CFG_BASE), keep the TSC's rate whatever its
          P-state definitions say, and load none of its microcode.
        - Intel: set the XSS-exiting bitmap where XSAVES is enabled.
        - Keep device interrupts off APIC ID FFh, the broadcast.
        - Give the OS memory with no regard to where the firmware's own memory
          types were, so that it has no holes there.

    Miscellaneous:
        - The OS gets only the CPU features, MSRs, and XSAVE components that
          ReBIOS knows, the model-specific MSRs by the processor model's or
          family's tables in Intel's and AMD's manuals. Those it does not
          support yet stay hidden, such as FRED, user interrupts, SGX, and
          memory encryption.
        - Hide the HPET from the OS where its timers can deliver interrupts
          through the FSB, as messages that would go wherever the OS pointed
          them.
        - Performance counters count only the OS's own work.
        - The profile names the exits of VMX instructions.
        - Cite the newest revisions of the documents and projects that the code
          follows.

2026-10-18  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.2.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.1.0:

    New features:
        - The OS now gets ACPI tables that ReBIOS generates, in ACPI 1.0 AML
          that even Windows 98 understands, rather than copies of the
          firmware's. Their methods call into ReBIOS, which runs the firmware's
          AML itself, and so the OS still sees the machine's devices and their
          resources, gets their events and notifications, the embedded
          controller's included, and powers off. Windows 98 installs and runs
          in ACPI mode, and the memory the firmware keeps for its AML no longer
          breaks up the OS's.
        - The ReBIOS USB stick: `make usb` builds an image for a USB stick that
          runs ReBIOS and boots the computer's other drives, which then need no
          FAT partition for ReBIOS. Releases ship it as `rebios-usb.img`.
        - New `boot_drive` setting, for the drive the legacy BIOS boots first:
          the one ReBIOS was started from (`self`, the default), one ReBIOS
          picks (`auto`: drives with something to boot first, internal ones,
          then CDs and DVDs, then external ones), or one named by its UEFI
          device path or by part of its model name.
        - Lay out PCI memory anew for the OS, without moving BARs on the
          hardware: BARs of 256 MiB or less come below 4 GiB, larger ones stay
          above it for 64-bit OSes, and the OS's memory below 4 GiB reaches up
          to those BARs, even where the firmware ends low memory well below
          them. The OS gets MTRRs that match.
        - Emulate the A20 gate of port 92h and of the keyboard controller:
          while the OS keeps it closed, every odd MiB of memory mirrors the
          even one below it, as on a PC.
        - New `profile` setting, which logs every 10 seconds how often and why
          the OS makes ReBIOS step in. A debugging aid.

    Bug fixes:
        - Carry out the OS's hardware task switches on Intel processors, which
          leave them to the hypervisor. ReBIOS used to stop.
        - Let addresses with nothing behind them read as all ones, and drop
          writes to them, as on a PC. ReBIOS used to stop.
        - Reserve, in the OS's memory map, the address space below 4 GiB that
          no memory backs, so that the OS places no device there.
        - Emulate string I/O, such as REP OUTSW, to the ports ReBIOS emulates.
          Windows 7's boot graphics on the emulated VGA stopped ReBIOS.
        - Emulated VGA: build the Video BIOS for the 386, so that the x86
          emulators that run Video BIOSes, such as 64-bit Windows', find no
          instructions they lack. Windows 7 x64 showed a black screen after its
          boot animation.
        - Emulated VGA: return the VBE mode list in the caller's information
          block, where 64-bit Windows' emulator finds it. Windows 7 x64 ran in
          16 colours.
        - Raise the OS's single-step traps after the instructions ReBIOS
          carries out for it, and after each iteration of a string instruction,
          as the processor would. Debuggers lost control stepping over trapped
          port I/O, CPUID, or emulated memory accesses.
        - Let pending interrupts in as soon as the OS has taken an exception
          that ReBIOS raised, rather than at its next unrelated exit.
        - Intel: block NMIs again for an IRET from an NMI handler that an EPT
          violation interrupted, as the SDM requires.
        - Intel: hide from CPUID the instructions VMX would not let the OS run:
          RDTSCP, RDPID, INVPCID, XSAVES, WAITPKG, PCONFIG, MSRLIST, and
          PBNDKB.
        - AMD Family 10h and 12h: work around erratum 383, as AMD's revision
          guide asks of hypervisors.
        - AMD Family 15h Models 10h-1Fh: work around IOMMU erratum 746, and
          wait for the IOMMU event log entries its tail pointer ran ahead of
          (erratum 732).
        - Intel: keep up the DMA translation the firmware left on for the
          integrated graphics of recent generations, which can hang when it is
          turned off, and map only small pages on Sandy Bridge's IOMMUs, which
          do not implement the large ones they claim.
        - Connect the storage controllers the firmware left alone, as with fast
          boot enabled, so that their drives can be ordered.
        - Allocate the loader's memory below 4 GiB where there is room, as some
          firmware leaves memory above 4 GiB out of its page tables.
        - Refuse memory maps whose descriptors are too small to read, and VT-d
          units with nothing answering at their registers, rather than misread
          them.

    Miscellaneous:
        - ReBIOS is now licensed under the GNU GPL version 2 only, rather than
          version 3 or later, so that GPLv2-only code, such as Linux's, can be
          used. SeaBIOS, under the GNU LGPL version 3, remains a separate
          program, which ReBIOS carries and runs as the legacy BIOS.
        - Emulated VGA: VBE modes in the screen's own size and format draw
          straight to the screen.
        - Cache VMCS reads and writes between VM entries, which speeds up
          ReBIOS when it runs nested, as under KVM.
        - `make run` runs QEMU with nested KVM and an Intel IOMMU.
        - Follow the latest PicoEFI C template: relocations are packed where
          the linker can, and builds cope with distribution flags that add
          `-fexceptions` and with Clang drivers that ignore `-fuse-ld`.
        - Update uACPI to its latest revision, beyond 7.0.0, and the PicoEFI,
          freestanding-c-hdrs, and freestanding-lgrt (formerly cc-runtime)
          subprojects.
        - Releases come with this ChangeLog, which their notes link to.

